<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Warri Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1480 CE – 1884 CE (approx.) · capital Ode-Itsekiri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Itsekiri kingdom of the western Niger delta, founded by a Benin prince, whose kings adopted Catholicism and traded with Europe from the 16th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Itsekiri tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ginuwa">Ginuwa</a>, a prince of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> around 1480 with followers and settled among the creeks of the western delta. His descendants took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olu-of-warri">Olu</a>, and the capital was established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ode-itsekiri">Ode-Itsekiri</a>, known to Europeans as Big Warri.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Warri's kings controlled trade between the interior and European ships. Portuguese missionaries converted the court, and around 1600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dom-domingos-of-warri">Olu Dom Domingos</a>, educated in Portugal, married a Portuguese woman. Warri dealt in slaves, cloth and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akengbuwa">Olu Akengbuwa</a> in 1848, rival houses could not agree on a successor and governors of the river held power.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The British signed a protection treaty in 1884 and in 1894 destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebrohimi">Ebrohimi</a>, the town of the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nana-olomu">Nana Olomu</a>. Warri became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a>. The kingship was restored in 1936, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olu-of-warri">Olu of Warri</a> still reigns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/warri-kingdom.jpg" alt="Warri Kingdom" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Nicolas Bellin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1765_Bonne_Map_of_West_Africa,_the_Gulf_of_Guinea,_and_Benin_-_Geographicus_-_GulfofGuinee-bellin-1765.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1480 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ode-Itsekiri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ginuwa, Dom Domingos, Akengbuwa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Itsekiri traditional religion and Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Itsekiri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Warri, Itsekiri Kingdom, Iwere</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=warri-kingdom" aria-label="Warri Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4839 1971 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Warri Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5159 2227L5155 2197M5159 2227L5175 2243"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5155 2197h0M5175 2243h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5159 2227h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5159 2227h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=warri-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
